<section class="chapter" id="medieval" style="--acc:#C9A24B;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#C9A24B 14%,white)">
<div class="chapter-head">
<span class="chapter-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M8 30 L8 16 Q8 10 14 10 Q20 10 20 16 L20 30"></path><path d="M20 30 L20 16 Q20 10 26 10 Q32 10 32 16 L32 30"></path><circle cx="20" cy="7" r="2"></circle></svg></span>
<div>
<div class="chapter-eyebrow">Period 01 · c. 1000–1400</div>
<h2 class="chapter-title">Medieval</h2>
</div>
<p class="chapter-claim">Form follows sacred text, then learned pattern. The liturgical word governs the line; as notation develops, composers add audible systems for coordinating several layers.</p>
</div>

<p class="hero-marker">Hero form · Plainchant into organum</p>
<p class="body">A sacred line becomes the ground for a new world of simultaneous voices. Plainchant presents a single, text-shaped melody without regular meter — complete for its liturgical purpose. Organum then preserves that chant as a sustained foundation while a faster upper voice unfolds above it, creating a new conception of coordinated musical space and an early foundation for Western polyphony.</p>

