<p class="section-label">Related form · grouped by function</p>
<div class="fn-group">Coordinating independent cycles</div>
<div class="related-wrap">
<details class="related">
<summary><span class="rf-fn">Ars Nova · c. 14th c.</span><span class="rf-name">Isorhythmic motet</span></summary>
<div class="rf-body">
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> In the fourteenth-century Ars Nova, composers could repeat a rhythmic pattern (<em>talea</em>) and a melodic sequence (<em>color</em>) across different spans, especially in the tenor, while upper voices carried other texts and rhythms.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> Isorhythm makes abstract planning audible across long durations — a culture coordinating independent cycles rather than simply stacking voices above chant. It supplies the later-medieval bridge into the Renaissance.</p>
<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Listening study</span><span class="rm-work">Isorhythmic motet</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">Track the lower <em>talea</em> cycle even when the pitch <em>color</em> and upper voices do not restart with it. Four rhythm cycles and three pitch cycles cross at different points, then realign at the close.</p>
