<section class="chapter" id="renaissance" style="--acc:#B54C3A;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#B54C3A 13%,white)">
<div class="chapter-head">
<span class="chapter-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M6 26 Q 13 14 20 26 Q 27 38 34 26"></path><path d="M6 18 Q 13 6 20 18 Q 27 30 34 18" opacity="0.5"></path></svg></span>
<div><div class="chapter-eyebrow">Period 02 · Renaissance &amp; Reformation</div><h2 class="chapter-title">Renaissance</h2></div>
<p class="chapter-claim">Form as balanced polyphonic discourse — imitation and recurring borrowed material make independent voices sound like one coherent whole.</p>
</div>
<p class="hero-marker">Hero form · Imitative motet</p>
<p class="body">Independent voices share one idea without surrendering their equality. A motet sets a sacred Latin text; a melodic shape tied to a phrase enters in one voice and is taken up by the others, producing overlapping entries before the music moves on to a new idea. Imitation creates unity without a single dominant melody — the period's clearest musical analogue for balanced, humanist discourse.</p>
<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Hero study</span><span class="rm-work">Imitative motet &amp; through-composed madrigal</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">Follow the first melodic shape and hear it pass from voice to voice; notice that no entry becomes a permanent “lead.” The same player also carries the <em>madrigal</em> reading below — the same craft turned toward the meaning of words.</p>
