<div class="fn-group">The Reformation: who sings, and in what language</div>
<div class="related-wrap">
<details class="related">
<summary><span class="rf-fn">Reformation · congregational</span><span class="rf-name">Lutheran chorale</span></summary>
<div class="rf-body">
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> A chorale is a vernacular, usually strophic congregational hymn: a memorable tune and repeated stanza structure that a whole congregation can sing.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> The chorale changes <em>who</em> sings and who understands the words — a vehicle for vernacular worship and collective confession, the necessary Reformation counterpole to elite Latin polyphony. This same tune returns, elaborated, in the Baroque chorale prelude.</p>
<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Listening study</span><span class="rm-work">Lutheran chorale &amp; Baroque chorale prelude</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">Hear how the stable tune and phrase pattern make repeated stanzas singable by a congregation. The player also holds the organ <em>chorale prelude</em> reading — the same melody elaborated a century later.</p>
