<section class="chapter" id="baroque" style="--acc:#B07028;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#B07028 13%,white)">
<div class="chapter-head">
<span class="chapter-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M8 32 Q 8 8 20 8 Q 32 8 32 20"></path><path d="M14 32 Q 14 14 24 14"></path><circle cx="32" cy="24" r="2.4"></circle></svg></span>
<div><div class="chapter-eyebrow">Period 03 · The Baroque</div><h2 class="chapter-title">Baroque</h2></div>
<p class="chapter-claim">Form as controlled drama — extravagance held inside strong frameworks: a subject through counterpoint, a bass beneath changing emotion, a refrain between solo adventures.</p>
</div>
<p class="hero-marker">Hero form · Fugue</p>
<p class="body">One subject generates disciplined abundance as it passes through a web of voices. A fugue begins when one voice states a subject and the others enter successively with related answers; episodes, renewed entries, inversion, and stretto build momentum. A fugue is a <em>process</em>, not a floor plan — nothing is set aside and “developed later,” because the subject is being worked continuously from the first bar to the last.</p>
<p class="body">That makes a particular demand on its audience: fugue asks you to <strong>track a single idea through simultaneous voices</strong>. There is no home key to feel your way back to, no second theme to wait for — you follow one shape as it accumulates company, and your sense of arrival comes from density rather than resolution. It rewards the concentrated, initiated listener of a church loft or a connoisseur's keyboard — and it is a direct bridge to Milton's extended, interwoven syntax.</p>
<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Hero study</span><span class="rm-work">Compact fugue</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">Hold the subject in memory from its first bare statement, then count its reentries as counterpoint accumulates around it. The lanes are voices, not pitches: watch one idea occupy all three.</p>
