<summary><span class="rf-fn">Opera · the stage</span><span class="rf-name">Recitative &amp; da capo aria</span></summary>
<div class="rf-body">
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> Speech-like <em>recitative</em> carries dialogue and plot with flexible rhythm and spare accompaniment. The <em>da capo aria</em> answers with an ABA design — an affect stated, a contrasting middle, then a return “from the head,” traditionally ornamented.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> Recitative let Baroque opera behave like heightened speech and move the drama; the aria suspends the action so a character can inhabit and transform one emotional state. The same music returns — but not as an identical experience.</p>
