<div class="fn-group">Departure and return, nested</div>
<div class="related-wrap">
<details class="related">
<summary><span class="rf-fn">Dance · tonal journey</span><span class="rf-name">Binary dance &amp; dance suite</span></summary>
<div class="rf-body">
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> Two repeated halves — A and B — organize a stylized dance: the first moves away from the home key, the second travels and returns. A <em>suite</em> gathers several such dances (allemande, courante, sarabande, gigue…) into one key-unified whole.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> Binary form is a compact tonal journey and an ancestor of sonata procedure; the suite teaches nested form — repeated halves inside each dance, contrast and continuity across the sequence.</p>
<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Listening study</span><span class="rm-work">Binary dance &amp; Baroque suite</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">Hear each half repeat, then compare the first half's departure with the second half's return — and hear both levels at once across the suite.</p>
