<p><strong>What it is.</strong> Comic-opera ensembles — especially act finales — bring several characters with different motives into simultaneous musical action; new entrances accelerate the plot and thicken the texture.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> One of the Enlightenment's most socially revealing forms: servants and elites share one stage, and the ensemble exposes competing perspectives at once. It is the musical counterpart to comedy of manners — a crucial link to <em>Pride and Prejudice</em>.</p>
