<section class="chapter" id="romanticism" style="--acc:#3D5A6D;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#3D5A6D 15%,white)">
<div class="chapter-head">
<span class="chapter-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M6 28 Q 14 4 20 20 Q 26 36 34 12"></path><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="1.8"></circle></svg></span>
<div><div class="chapter-eyebrow">Period 05 · Romanticism</div><h2 class="chapter-title">Romanticism</h2></div>
<p class="chapter-claim">Form as transformed identity — a work stays coherent while emotion and individual voice overflow inherited proportions.</p>
</div>
<p class="hero-marker">Hero form · Thematic transformation / idée fixe</p>
<p class="body">A Romantic theme remains itself by carrying the history of everything that changed it, prioritizing expression of rigid adherence to established musical forms. A recognizable idea recurs across sections or movements with altered rhythm, harmony, tempo, register, or orchestration; in Berlioz's <em>Symphonie fantastique</em>, the idée fixe represents the beloved and changes as the protagonist's perception changes.</p>
<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Hero study</span><span class="rm-work">Thematic transformation &amp; free fantasy</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">Ask not only “did the theme return?” but “what has this experience turned the theme into?” The same player also carries the <em>free fantasy</em> reading — form as apparent spontaneity.</p>
