<div class="fn-group">The same frame, and the abandoned frame</div>
<div class="related-wrap">
<details class="related">
<summary><span class="rf-fn">Song · the Lied</span><span class="rf-name">Strophic &amp; through-composed song</span></summary>
<div class="rf-body">
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> In a <em>strophic</em> song every stanza uses the same music; in a <em>through-composed</em> song new music follows the poem continuously, responding to narrative and atmosphere.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> Repetition can deepen meaning — an unchanged frame makes the differences between verses newly audible; through-composition makes song capable of dramatic narration and fine-grained textual interpretation.</p>
<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Listening study</span><span class="rm-work">Strophic &amp; through-composed song</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">In the strophic reading, notice how new words recolor the same music; in the through-composed reading, notice how the music changes because the story cannot stay in one frame.</p>
