<p class="bridge"><strong>Bridge forward</strong>Modernism doesn't discard Romantic coherence — it redistributes it into blocks, cycles, rows, rhythm, and timbre that no longer depend on one shared tonal narrative.</p>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="modernism" style="--acc:#A03828;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#A03828 12%,white)">
<div class="chapter-head">
<span class="chapter-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><rect x="7" y="9" width="11" height="11"></rect><rect x="22" y="14" width="11" height="11" opacity="0.6"></rect><rect x="12" y="24" width="11" height="8" opacity="0.4"></rect></svg></span>
<div><div class="chapter-eyebrow">Period 06 · Modernism</div><h2 class="chapter-title">Modernism</h2></div>
<p class="chapter-claim">Multiple systems, no single path — coherence can come from juxtaposed blocks, a repeated harmonic/lyric cycle, or a systematic pitch order without a tonic.</p>
</div>
<p class="hero-marker">Two Modernist answers · coequal</p>
<p class="body">Modernism all but abandons musical tradition, making the raw material of musical expression its explicit subject matter. Harmony, melody, and rhythm are modified almost beyond recognition whenever and however such modifications serve the expressive needs or intellectual goals of the composer. European concert composers may juxtapose self-contained blocks of atonal sound unified only by timbre or rhythm; African American blues practice builds expressive freedom inside a durable harmonic and lyric cycle.</p>

<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Answer one</span><span class="rm-work">Modernist block / mosaic construction</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">A block form juxtaposes relatively self-contained fields — a new block may interrupt rather than grow from the last. Rhythm, register, and instrumentation can define a block as strongly as melody. Identify each sound-block by its whole profile, then follow the sequence of cuts and returns rather than a smooth transition.</p>
