<summary><span class="rf-fn">Row · non-tonal</span><span class="rf-name">Twelve-tone organization</span></summary>
<div class="rf-body">
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> A composer orders the twelve pitch classes into a row and derives material from its prime, inversion, retrograde, and retrograde-inversion forms, often transposed. The technique does not dictate rhythm, register, timbre, or genre.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> Twelve-tone method offers systematic pitch coherence without a tonic hierarchy — non-tonal music that is organized, not random. It is historically distinct from earlier free atonality.</p>
<div class="realmusic">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Listening study</span><span class="rm-work">Twelve-tone organization</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">Follow the recurrence and transformation of an ordered pitch identity; don't wait for a home key, and don't assume every new sound is unrelated.</p>
<div class="viz-embed">
