<!-- ============ CLOSING SYNTHESIS ============ -->
<section class="synthesis">
<p class="s-eyebrow">Closing synthesis</p>
<h2>Six ways of giving time a shape</h2>
<div class="arc" aria-label="The arc across six periods">
<span class="step"><span class="sn">Medieval</span>text &amp; ritual</span><span class="arw">→</span>
<span class="step"><span class="sn">Renaissance</span>interwoven voices</span><span class="arw">→</span>
<span class="step"><span class="sn">Baroque</span>recurrence &amp; contrast</span><span class="arw">→</span>
<span class="step"><span class="sn">Enlightenment</span>tonal architecture</span><span class="arw">→</span>
<span class="step"><span class="sn">Romanticism</span>transformed identity</span><span class="arw">→</span>
<span class="step"><span class="sn">Modernism</span>plural systems</span>
</div>
<p class="close-quote">Once a listener can hear repetition, contrast, transformation, and return, form no longer depends on one historical grammar. <strong>A chant follows sacred words; a fugue follows a subject through voices; a sonata makes a tonal argument; a blues performance renews a cycle; a mosaic remembers through cuts.</strong> Each gives time a shape, but none owns the idea of musical order — later forms do not replace earlier ones; they reuse, revive, and reinterpret them.</p>
</section>

